To test the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method, we need to make it accessible to the test class. We can do this by applying the `make_virtual` transform to the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method.

Here's the corrected test code:

```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using System;

namespace Jellyfin.Networking.Manager.Tests
{
    public class NetworkManagerTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void OnNetworkAddressChanged_LogsNetworkAddressChangeDetected()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<NetworkManager>>();
            var configurationManagerMock = new Mock<IConfigurationManager>();
            var configurationMock = new Mock<IConfiguration>();
            var networkManager = new NetworkManager(configurationManagerMock.Object, configurationMock.Object, loggerMock.Object);

            // Act
            networkManager.OnNetworkAddressChanged(null, EventArgs.Empty);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(logger => logger.LogDebug("Network address change detected."), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

However, since the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method is private, we cannot directly call it in the test. We need to find another way to test its functionality.

One possible approach is to test the `NetworkChanged` event, which is triggered by the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method. We can subscribe to the `NetworkChanged` event and verify that it is triggered when the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method is called.

Here's an example of how we can modify the test to achieve this:

```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using System;

namespace Jellyfin.Networking.Manager.Tests
{
    public class NetworkManagerTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void OnNetworkAddressChanged_TriggerNetworkChangedEvent()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<NetworkManager>>();
            var configurationManagerMock = new Mock<IConfigurationManager>();
            var configurationMock = new Mock<IConfiguration>();
            var networkManager = new NetworkManager(configurationManagerMock.Object, configurationMock.Object, loggerMock.Object);
            var networkChangedCalled = false;

            networkManager.NetworkChanged += (sender, args) => networkChangedCalled = true;

            // Act
            networkManager.OnNetworkAddressChanged(null, EventArgs.Empty);

            // Assert
            Assert.True(networkChangedCalled);
        }
    }
}
```

In this modified test, we subscribe to the `NetworkChanged` event and set a flag `networkChangedCalled` to `true` when the event is triggered. We then verify that the flag is `true` after calling the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method.

Note that this approach assumes that the `OnNetworkAddressChanged` method triggers the `NetworkChanged` event. If this is not the case, we may need to modify the test accordingly.
